## Completed

B2’s lower dollar P&L is **not explained mainly by an underused $1,000 stop-risk budget**. B2 used a median 97.8% of that budget over the full history and 95.6% after MNQ launched. Its median size after launch was 6 MNQ, or 0.6 NQ-equivalent contracts, so lower point exposure did reduce dollar swings.

After launch, B2 still had weaker results than B0: $104,615 vs. $169,969 net P&L, 49.3% vs. 51.7% win rate, and 1.17 vs. 1.87 daily Sharpe. In 2026, both strategies lost money; B2 lost fewer dollars, but its daily Sharpe was slightly worse.

The report includes risk-unit results, Calmar, paired moving-block Monte Carlo, sizing analysis, and ledger limitations. The bootstrap favors B0’s Sharpe in nearly all post-launch resamples. It does not show that B2 will outperform or underperform in future regimes.

